  {:criterion :fetched-bodies-are-validated :status :met :evidence "Added after the first JP corpus shipped 8,548 identical HTML 404 pages that laws.e-gov.go.jp served with HTTP 200 -- accepted because the fetcher checked only resp.ok. Nothing downstream caught it: the file count, the failure log (empty), the index row count and every quality threshold all looked correct. It surfaced only because git-annex deduplicated 9,537 files into 989 keys. Fetchers now pass a :validate predicate into lib/fetch-buffer (a failing 2xx body is retried on the same backoff as a 5xx) AND re-validate files already on disk before skipping them, without which a resumed run preserves the saved error pages forever. Re-fetched corpus: 9,523 texts, all sha256 distinct, 1,229,999,063 bytes -- 2.4x the bogus one. The same duplicate-ratio check was run against the other three sources and found no contamination (EU 1,114 / UK 3,267 / US 20,852 bill versions, all distinct). General rule this encodes: a status code is not evidence about a body, and a matching row count is not evidence of health -- check the duplicate ratio of the content."}
  {:criterion :no-text-stored-in-this-repo :status :met :evidence "data/quality-report.edn :quality/text-bytes-held-here is gated at a maximum of 0 and asserted by test/query_contract.clj. This is the surviving half of the old ':quality/ingested-full-text pinned to 0' invariant: that single number conflated 'this repo holds no text' (still true, still enforced) with 'no text has been ingested anywhere' (false since 2026-08-04). Keeping one number would have required lying about one of the two."}
  {:criterion :dependency-graph :status :met :evidence "646,468 law-to-law edges across 13 relation kinds (amends / repeals / implicitly-repeals / based-on / completes / corrects / codified-as / cites / became-law / identical-measure / companion-measure / procedurally-related / related-measure). Every edge derives from structured upstream metadata, never from natural-language inference over the text. queries/examples.edn publishes :what-amends, :what-repeals, :legal-basis-closure (recursive), :dangling-edges. test/corpus_contract.clj asserts edge DIRECTION, not just count: the Constitution of Japan has never been amended, so a non-empty :what-amends result on it means a from/to inversion -- a bug row counts cannot see."}
  {:criterion :dangling-edges-disclosed :status :met :evidence ":law.rel/resolved? is stored per edge; 370,551 of 646,468 edges resolve and the remaining 275,917 point outside this projection (mostly EU amending acts long since absorbed). They are kept, not dropped: an edge recording that something was repealed is the useful case. queries/examples.edn :dangling-edges makes the set enumerable rather than leaving the graph to look complete."}
  {:criterion :corpus-catalog-join :status :met :evidence "bin/build-corpus.cljs rewrites each dataset's :law/source-id to the CATALOG's id at the boundary, so :law/source-id joins :legal-source/id. Without it, 'under what licence may I use this text?' (queries/examples.edn :corpus-source-license) silently returns nothing -- test/corpus_contract.clj asserts the join resolves to :tier/a for a known law."}
