  {:criterion :ci-detects-a-tampered-shard :status :met :evidence "The sha256 check closes what the GitHub Actions design structurally could not. Actions only ever read values that were already committed; nothing regenerated the files, so `git diff --exit-code -- data` stayed silent and a hand-edited shard that remained internally consistent would have passed. This was recorded as :ci-rebuilds-corpus :known-gap on 2026-08-04-05 and is now closed by a different mechanism than the one proposed then."}
  {:criterion :ci-rederives-corpus :status :known-gap :evidence "Nothing RE-DERIVES data/corpus/** from the locked source datasets in CI; that needs their index/ trees on a fleet node. The sha256 gate makes tampering detectable but does not make the derivation reproducible. Also not done: the EU/UK/US shards are verified by sha256 and entity count only, not parsed for semantics (646,468 edges take minutes to read with edamame). The Datascript assertions do not run on the fleet gate either, but NOT for the reason first recorded here: that said the nodes have no outbound HTTPS, which was one 2026-07-26 measurement of one node (zebulun) inherited from fleet-ci README. Measured directly on 2026-08-05, all ten reachable nodes return 200 from repo1.maven.org, so a maven dep would resolve. They stay in the local contract (clojure -M test/query_contract.clj) because the sha256/count/edge-direction checks need no Datalog engine and run faster, not because the fleet cannot host them."}
